; The canonical change from S5 to S6.
;
; S5 |- S6. A type says what a program may do, not only what it is.
;
; S2 lets a type mention a value. S6 lets a declaration say what happens: what
; a definition needs permission for, and how many times a parameter may be
; used. Neither is a comment.
;
; A capability does not travel. A definition that calls something needing `io`
; must declare `io` itself, so permission cannot be acquired by going one call
; deeper.
;
; A multiplicity is counted along every path a match can take, and a rejection
; names the path. `once` is exactly one use on every path, `affine` at most
; one, `erased` none at all -- which is what makes an erased parameter safe to
; remove.
;
; Removing it is a pass over the compiler's core rather than a second reader:
; the definition stops taking it, every application stops supplying it, and
; the indices of everything else move to match. What the program computes does
; not.
(change
  (kind foundation)
  (name "S5->S6")
  (predecessor (foundation-of "foundations/S5"))
  (delta-meta (file "changes/S5-S6/delta-meta.canon"))
  (delta-grammar (file "changes/S5-S6/delta-grammar.canon"))
  (feature
    (feature
      (name "effects and multiplicities")
      (programs [
        (file "features/strata/effect.generated.meta")
      ])
      (sources [
        (text-file "features/strata/effect.meta")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.grammar")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.meta")
        (text-file "strata/lib/effects.strata")
      ]))))
